    "question": "What is the difference between raster and vector graphics?",
    "slug": "what-is-the-difference-between-raster-and-vector-graphics",
    "shortDescription": "1. [REPORTED / SOURCE-LISTED] | Fresher / Entry-Level",
    "sampleAnswer": "Raster graphics are made from pixels, while vector graphics are defined by mathematical paths, curves, fills, and strokes. Raster images are resolution-dependent: enlarging them too much can make them appear blurry or pixelated. Vector artwork can generally be scaled to different sizes without losing geometric sharpness. Raster is commonly used for photographs and detailed pixel-based imagery. Vector is preferred for logos, icons, illustrations, and artwork that must work across many sizes. In production, the choice should be based on the content and output rather than assuming one format is always better.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: 1. [REPORTED / SOURCE-LISTED]\n\nLevel: Fresher / Entry-Level\n\nAnswer:\nRaster graphics are made from pixels, while vector graphics are defined by mathematical paths, curves, fills, and strokes. Raster images are resolution-dependent: enlarging them too much can make them appear blurry or pixelated. Vector artwork can generally be scaled to different sizes without losing geometric sharpness. Raster is commonly used for photographs and detailed pixel-based imagery. Vector is preferred for logos, icons, illustrations, and artwork that must work across many sizes. In production, the choice should be based on the content and output rather than assuming one format is always better.\n\nExample:\nExample: A company logo should normally be maintained as vector artwork so it can be placed on a business card, website, billboard, or sign without becoming pixelated. A photograph used in a campaign is normally raster.\n\nInterview Tip:\nExplain your reasoning, not just the final visual result. Connect design choices to audience, objective, brand, usability, accessibility, production, or measurable outcomes.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nDesign fundamentals, visual judgment, problem-solving, portfolio ownership, communication, collaboration, production awareness, and the ability to defend decisions with objective reasoning.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nStart from the brief and audience; distinguish personal preference from design rationale; show iteration; communicate trade-offs; and protect quality and accessibility.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy did you choose that approach? What alternatives did you consider? What feedback did you receive? What would you change now? How did you validate the result?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nSaying “it looks better” without evidence; claiming team work as entirely your own; becoming defensive about feedback; ignoring production constraints; overlooking accessibility; or describing software skills without demonstrating how the tools solve design problems. ------------------------------------------------------------------------",
