    "sampleAnswer": "Clearly separate your own contribution from the team’s contribution. Explain what you were responsible for, which decisions you made, what you produced, and where you collaborated. If other people contributed to research, copy, strategy, photography, development, or production, acknowledge that rather than presenting the entire project as your individual work.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: 19. [REPORTED / SOURCE-LISTED]\n\nLevel: Fresher / Entry-Level\n\nAnswer:\nClearly separate your own contribution from the team’s contribution. Explain what you were responsible for, which decisions you made, what you produced, and where you collaborated. If other people contributed to research, copy, strategy, photography, development, or production, acknowledge that rather than presenting the entire project as your individual work.\n\nExample:\nExample: Take a real design brief, identify the audience and objective, show two or three alternatives, explain the decision criteria, then demonstrate how feedback or testing changed the final work.\n\nInterview Tip:\nExplain your reasoning, not just the final visual result. Connect design choices to audience, objective, brand, usability, accessibility, production, or measurable outcomes.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nDesign fundamentals, visual judgment, problem-solving, portfolio ownership, communication, collaboration, production awareness, and the ability to defend decisions with objective reasoning.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nStart from the brief and audience; distinguish personal preference from design rationale; show iteration; communicate trade-offs; and protect quality and accessibility.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy did you choose that approach? What alternatives did you consider? What feedback did you receive? What would you change now? How did you validate the result?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nSaying “it looks better” without evidence; claiming team work as entirely your own; becoming defensive about feedback; ignoring production constraints; overlooking accessibility; or describing software skills without demonstrating how the tools solve design problems. ------------------------------------------------------------------------",
