    "shortDescription": "28. [REPORTED / SOURCE-LISTED] | Intermediate",
    "sampleAnswer": "First understand the reason behind the feedback. The request may reveal a real issue even if the proposed solution is not ideal. Explain your concern using the brief, audience, accessibility, brand, or evidence. Offer an alternative that addresses the underlying need. If the final decision still differs from your preference, support the agreed direction professionally.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: 28. [REPORTED / SOURCE-LISTED]\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAnswer:\nFirst understand the reason behind the feedback. The request may reveal a real issue even if the proposed solution is not ideal. Explain your concern using the brief, audience, accessibility, brand, or evidence. Offer an alternative that addresses the underlying need. If the final decision still differs from your preference, support the agreed direction professionally.\n\nExample:\nExample: A stakeholder says a poster “doesn’t feel energetic.” Ask what outcome they expect, then explore whether the issue is color, imagery, typography, composition, or messaging instead of blindly changing every element.\n\nInterview Tip:\nExplain your reasoning, not just the final visual result. Connect design choices to audience, objective, brand, usability, accessibility, production, or measurable outcomes.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nDesign fundamentals, visual judgment, problem-solving, portfolio ownership, communication, collaboration, production awareness, and the ability to defend decisions with objective reasoning.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nStart from the brief and audience; distinguish personal preference from design rationale; show iteration; communicate trade-offs; and protect quality and accessibility.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy did you choose that approach? What alternatives did you consider? What feedback did you receive? What would you change now? How did you validate the result?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nSaying “it looks better” without evidence; claiming team work as entirely your own; becoming defensive about feedback; ignoring production constraints; overlooking accessibility; or describing software skills without demonstrating how the tools solve design problems. ------------------------------------------------------------------------",
