    "sampleAnswer": "Prioritize by business impact, deadline, dependency, effort, and risk. Confirm priorities when multiple requests conflict instead of silently trying to treat everything as equally urgent. Use a visible task list or project tracker, break work into milestones, communicate capacity early, and reserve time for review and production. This makes deadline risks visible before they become emergencies.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: 34. [REPORTED / SOURCE-LISTED]\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAnswer:\nPrioritize by business impact, deadline, dependency, effort, and risk. Confirm priorities when multiple requests conflict instead of silently trying to treat everything as equally urgent. Use a visible task list or project tracker, break work into milestones, communicate capacity early, and reserve time for review and production. This makes deadline risks visible before they become emergencies.\n\nExample:\nExample: With one day left, prioritize the approved core layout, correct dimensions, readable type, final assets, and production checks; defer optional decorative variations until the critical deliverable is safe.\n\nInterview Tip:\nExplain your reasoning, not just the final visual result. Connect design choices to audience, objective, brand, usability, accessibility, production, or measurable outcomes.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nDesign fundamentals, visual judgment, problem-solving, portfolio ownership, communication, collaboration, production awareness, and the ability to defend decisions with objective reasoning.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nStart from the brief and audience; distinguish personal preference from design rationale; show iteration; communicate trade-offs; and protect quality and accessibility.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy did you choose that approach? What alternatives did you consider? What feedback did you receive? What would you change now? How did you validate the result?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nSaying “it looks better” without evidence; claiming team work as entirely your own; becoming defensive about feedback; ignoring production constraints; overlooking accessibility; or describing software skills without demonstrating how the tools solve design problems. ------------------------------------------------------------------------",
