    "shortDescription": "59. [SCENARIO / SOURCE-LISTED] | Advanced / Senior / Scenario-Based",
    "sampleAnswer": "Translate the business problem into a communication/design objective. Define the audience, desired behavior, message, constraints, and success metric. Then design, test, and refine against those criteria. For example, if a company has low event registrations, the design problem may involve unclear value communication or weak visual hierarchy rather than simply needing a more attractive poster.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Source classification: 59. [SCENARIO / SOURCE-LISTED]\n\nLevel: Advanced / Senior / Scenario-Based\n\nAnswer:\nTranslate the business problem into a communication/design objective. Define the audience, desired behavior, message, constraints, and success metric. Then design, test, and refine against those criteria. For example, if a company has low event registrations, the design problem may involve unclear value communication or weak visual hierarchy rather than simply needing a more attractive poster.\n\nExample:\nExample: If a landing-page campaign has low signups, redesign the visual hierarchy and call-to-action treatment around the user journey, then compare conversion performance rather than judging success only by aesthetics.\n\nInterview Tip:\nExplain your reasoning, not just the final visual result. Connect design choices to audience, objective, brand, usability, accessibility, production, or measurable outcomes.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nDesign fundamentals, visual judgment, problem-solving, portfolio ownership, communication, collaboration, production awareness, and the ability to defend decisions with objective reasoning.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nStart from the brief and audience; distinguish personal preference from design rationale; show iteration; communicate trade-offs; and protect quality and accessibility.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy did you choose that approach? What alternatives did you consider? What feedback did you receive? What would you change now? How did you validate the result?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nSaying “it looks better” without evidence; claiming team work as entirely your own; becoming defensive about feedback; ignoring production constraints; overlooking accessibility; or describing software skills without demonstrating how the tools solve design problems. ------------------------------------------------------------------------",
